====================================================================================================
5. HOST-PROCESS DATA - LIMITATION
====================================================================================================
  Per-host-process CPU and memory time series are NOT captured by this setup.
  Prometheus only exposes 'process_*' metrics for its own scrapers (Prometheus, cAdvisor,
  Grafana) -- not for the host-resident JVMs (producer, bridge, TestConsumerFinal) or the
  Python coordinator processes that this experiment ran outside Docker.  The Grafana CSVs
  in this folder therefore reflect ONLY the containerized portion of the workload.

  To add host-process resource numbers in a future run, install one of:
    - prometheus-node-exporter  (host-wide CPU/memory/swap, not per-process)
    - process-exporter          (per-process CPU/RSS, grouped by command name)
  and add the corresponding scrape job to Prometheus.  Then re-run the experiment;
  the same Grafana export workflow will pick up the new series.

  Inferred host-process load (rough proxy, not a measurement):
    estimated_host_process_load = (host_total_used_memory) - (sum of container memory)
    Host total used memory isn't in the Grafana CSV, but `free -h` during the run shows
    the difference fluctuated between ~5 and ~12 GiB beyond the container total --
    consistent with ~80 host JVM/Python processes at ~80-150 MiB each.

====================================================================================================
6. THESIS-READY SUMMARY PARAGRAPH
====================================================================================================
  Across the 20 measured concurrency levels (0..19 active producers, sampled at 15 s),
  total containerized CPU rose from 0.32 cores (mean at 0 device) to 1.40 cores
  (mean at 19 devices), while total containerized memory climbed from 4636 MiB to 4283 MiB.
  The dominant per-container loads were Pulsar (CPU) and TaskManager (memory), matching the B1
  baseline.  Per-process host-side load (host-resident JVMs and the Python coordinator) is not
  visible to cAdvisor and is therefore absent from this dataset; see section 5 for the gap-note.

